    {
      "id": "GPL-01",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-good-gram-phase",
      "title": "Four norm-one phases give an integral inverse",
      "statement": "At every odd finite place where d, Q and β are units and P is integral, for each integral t in the quadratic algebra at least one phase λ among ±1 and ±(P+Qθ) makes β−conj(λ)t an integral unit.",
      "hypotheses": "Quadratic CM extension E/F with trace-zero generator θ, θ²=d≠0, β∈F×, α=P+Qθ and P²−dQ²=1 with Q≠0. Local coefficient ring is the full ring of integers, including the product of valuation rings in the split case.",
      "correspondence": "The source shows the four base norms generate the unit ideal over the base algebra. The displayed residue-field proof is the valuation-ring specialization of its two norm differences. The source wrapper allows arbitrary unit β by applying the rational-scalar result to β⁻¹t and the target 1. The split calculation at 5 illustrates why invertibility must hold in both factors.",
